  "worked_solution": "We multiply both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+3)x$ and get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+9=(Ax+B)x+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+9 & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ 9 & =3C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us an equation for $A$ in terms of $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+9 & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(3)({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ 11 & =A+B+12 \\\\ A & =-B-1.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us another equation for $A$ in terms of $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)^{2}+9 & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(3)((-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ 11 & =A-B+12 \\\\ A & =B-1.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the equations $(1)$ and $(2)$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-B-1 & =B-1 \\\\ -2B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we use our result for $B$ in equation $(2).$ We get\n\n$$\nA=(0)-1=-1.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1,$ $B=0,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\nA+B+C=-1+0+3=2.\n$$",
